The philosophical spine of the full course:

Memory — what actually exists?
Pointers — how do we reach it?
Ownership — who is responsible for it?
Moves — how does responsibility change?
Aliasing & borrowing — who else may access it?
Lifetimes — for how long is that access valid?
Mutation — who is permitted to change it?
Concurrency — what happens when access occurs simultaneously?
Types & invariants — which program states should be representable?
unsafe — when does the programmer take the proof obligation back?

That is no longer really a “Rust syntax course.”

